.\" roff; cmake/driftwire-cli installs it gzipped, and the macOS archive
.\" (tools/mac/macpack.py cli) carries it for Homebrew, so it says where macOS
.\" differs. Check it with
.\"   man -l packaging/linux/driftwire.1
.TH DRIFTWIRE 1 2026-10-25 "Driftwire" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
driftwire \- send files to a computer nearby, or anywhere with a code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B driftwire
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B driftwire send
.IR PATH ...
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR NAME ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B driftwire receive
.RB [ \-\-once ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B driftwire code
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B driftwire devices
.br
.B driftwire
.BR \-\-help " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B driftwire
is Driftwire in a terminal: it sends files to another computer on the same
network, or to anyone, anywhere, who gives you a code. The other computer runs
.B driftwire
too, or MediaViewer with its Driftwire add-on (Windows and macOS).
.PP
It is interactive only. Nothing is sent and nothing is saved until the person
at the terminal answers: a receiver types its answer to every offer, a sender
types the code the receiver shows. There is no option to accept by itself, and
.B driftwire
refuses to start the session when its input or output is not a terminal.
.PP
While the session runs, this computer is visible to Driftwire nearby, by its
name, and can be sent to. When the session closes, it is not: there is no
receiving in the background.
.PP
Each transfer is sealed end to end. Every file is checked when it arrives;
a file that does not match what was sent is not kept. A receive that stops
part way (the network went, the sender closed) keeps what arrived and resumes
when the same sender sends the same files again.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B driftwire
Opens the session: the computers nearby, a prompt for files, and the
commands below.
.TP
.BI "driftwire send " PATH ...
Opens the session with these files, or one folder, ready to send. Pick a
computer with the arrow keys and press Enter. It closes when that send ends:
sent, declined, cancelled or failed.
.TP
.BI "driftwire send " PATH "... \-\-to " NAME
Sends to the first computer nearby called
.I NAME
as soon as it is heard (an exact name, ignoring case, then a unique
beginning of one). The code is still typed.
.TP
.B driftwire receive
Opens the session to receive.
.TP
.B driftwire receive \-\-once
Closes after one transfer: the first offer made to it, accepted or declined.
A computer that connects and leaves before offering anything does not count.
.TP
.B driftwire code
Opens the session with a code to receive from anywhere (this needs
.BR "Over the internet" ;
it asks before turning it on). It closes when the receive the code lets in
ends, or when the code is wrong, runs out or is stopped.
.TP
.B driftwire devices
Lists the devices this computer remembers, and exits. It needs no terminal.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Prints the usage and exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Prints the version and exits.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-to " DIR"
Saves received files in
.I DIR
for this run only. The folder kept for later is set with
.B /save\-to
in the session.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
The name other computers see, for this run only. Without it, the name is
the computer's pretty hostname
.RB ( PRETTY_HOSTNAME
in
.IR /etc/machine\-info ,
as
.B hostnamectl
sets it), else its hostname; on macOS, the computer's name in Sharing
settings.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Lines only: no colour, no cursor movement, and questions as plain lines,
for screen readers and dumb terminals. Also when
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-color ", " \-\-no\-colour
No colour. Also when
.B NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Shows what Driftwire logs. Its log never holds a file name, a path, an
address or a code.
.PP
No code, check digits or relay key is ever an argument: they would stay in
the shell's history and be visible to other users in
.BR ps (1).
They are typed or pasted in the session, and
.B "driftwire code 123456"
is an error.
.SH THE SESSION
.SS Sending
Type or paste paths and press Enter to add them; a terminal pastes files
dropped onto it as paths. Quotes, backslash escapes,
.BR ~ ,
.B file://
links and the wildcards
.BR * ,
.B ?
and
.B [...]
work as in a shell; relative paths start in the current folder. A send is
either files or one folder.
.PP
.B Nearby
lists the computers that have Driftwire open on the same network. Choose one
with the arrow keys and press Enter on an empty prompt. Its screen shows a
six-digit code; type it. It goes by itself at the sixth digit. Three wrong
codes end the attempt.
.SS Receiving
When someone nearby sends, the session shows a code in large digits: they
type it on their screen. Then the offer: who, how many files, how large, and
where they will be saved.
.TP
.BR 1 ", " y " or Enter"
Accept, and save to the folder shown.
.TP
.B 2
Accept and save somewhere else (a folder you type; Tab completes).
.TP
.BR 3 ", " n " or Esc"
Decline.
.PP
An offer, the check to compare and the question to remember take an answer
once they have been on screen a moment with nothing typed: keys that were
being typed when one appeared are not answers. Esc always is. An offer with
no answer for three minutes is declined. Press Esc twice to cancel a transfer
under way.
.SS From anywhere
.B /code
makes a code to give someone: four words, or groups of letters, and a link.
.B /copy
copies the code and
.B /copy link
the link (through the terminal, OSC 52, where it allows it). The person
sending pastes it into
.BR /to\-code ,
and both screens show the same six digits: check them before sending or
accepting.
.SS Remembering
After a transfer,
.B driftwire
may offer to remember the other computer, so the two can send to each other
later over the internet with no code. Nearby, the one remembering types a
code the other shows. Not on macOS, where nothing is kept to remember with
(see
.BR FILES ).
.SS Commands
Type
.B /
for the list, filtered as you type.
.TP
.BI /send " PATH..."
Add files to send.
.TP
.BI /folder " DIR"
Send a folder.
.TP
.B /files
List the files ready to send.
.TP
.B /clear
Clear them.
.TP
.B /code
Receive from anywhere: make a code to give someone.
.TP
.BR /copy " [" link ]
Copy the code, or its link, to the clipboard.
.TP
.B /to\-code
Send to a code someone gave you (pasted, never an argument).
.TP
.B /devices
List remembered devices, numbered.
.TP
.BI /forget " N"
Forget device
.I N
(it asks first).
.TP
.BI /mine " N " on|off
This is my computer: it may relay for me.
.TP
.B /unfinished
List receives that stopped part way;
.BI /discard " N"
drops one.
.TP
.BI /save\-to " DIR"
Where received files go, kept for later.
.TP
.BR /internet ", " /dht ", " /handover ", " /relay " on|off"
Send and receive over the internet; find each other through the public DHT;
continue over the internet when Wi-Fi drops; use your other computers to
relay. Each is off until turned on, and says what it means the first time.
.TP
.B /relay\-server
Your own relay server (its key is typed, never an argument).
.TP
.B /settings
Show the current settings.
.TP
.B /help
Commands and keys.
.TP
.BR /quit ", " /exit
Close Driftwire.
.SS Keys
.TP
.B Enter
Add what was typed, run a command, or (on an empty prompt) send to the
computer chosen.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a path, or a command in the list.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down
Move in the command list or in Nearby.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+P ", " Ctrl+N
What was typed before.
.TP
.B Esc
Close the command list, clear the prompt, then decline or cancel what is
under way.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Clear the prompt; twice, close Driftwire (during a transfer, cancel it and
close).
.TP
.B Ctrl+D
Close Driftwire (on an empty prompt).
.TP
.B Ctrl+L
Draw everything again.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z
Suspend;
.BR fg (1)
brings it back.
.PP
The line keys of a shell work in the prompt: Home, End, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+E,
Ctrl+U, Ctrl+K, Ctrl+W and Alt+Left/Right.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
It ended normally (and with
.BR send ,
.B receive \-\-once
or
.BR code ,
the transfer went through).
.TP
.B 1
The transfer of
.BR send ,
.B receive \-\-once
or
.B code
did not go through: declined, cancelled or failed.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, no terminal, or it could not start (Driftwire is already open
in another terminal of the same user).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, no colour.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
means plain lines (as
.BR \-\-plain ).
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
On Linux, where the data folder is (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
On Linux, where
.I user\-dirs.dirs
is read for the Downloads folder
.RB ( XDG_DOWNLOAD_DIR ,
as
.BR xdg\-user\-dirs\-update (1)
writes it); else
.IR ~/Downloads .
.TP
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
On Linux, the session bus, for the Secret Service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-~/.local/state}/driftwire/
The data folder on Linux, made readable by its owner only.
.TP
.I "~/Library/Application Support/Driftwire/"
The data folder on macOS, the same.
.TP
.I .../driftwire/driftwire.json
The settings: the folder received files go to, the internet options.
.TP
.I .../driftwire/driftwire_resume.json
What is kept of a receive that stopped part way, so it can resume
.RB ( /unfinished ).
.TP
.I .../driftwire/driftwire_dht.dat
The DHT nodes last seen, when finding each other through the DHT is on.
.TP
.I .../driftwire/driftwire.json.lock
Held while a session runs: one session per user at a time.
.TP
.I ~/Downloads
Where received files go unless
.B /save\-to
or
.B \-\-save\-to
says otherwise. A file that is still arriving is a hidden
.I .NAME.mvtmp
beside it.
.PP
On Linux, this computer's identity and the devices it remembers are kept in the
freedesktop Secret Service (GNOME Keyring, KWallet, KeePassXC), under the
service
.BR "Driftwire CLI" ,
apart from MediaViewer's. Without a Secret Service, or with the keyring
locked (an SSH login, for one), each run has an identity of its own and
nothing is remembered. The Secret Service lets any program of the same user
read its items; each value is sealed so that one not written by Driftwire is
refused.
.PP
On macOS nothing is kept in a key store: each run has an identity of its
own, nothing is remembered, and
.B driftwire devices
lists none.
.SH FIREWALL
Driftwire nearby sends a beacon on UDP port 47613 (to 239.255.77.83 and the
subnet broadcast) and lists the computers whose beacons it hears; a sender
then connects to the receiver over TCP, on a port the receiver's system
picks. So seeing who is nearby needs the beacon let in, and receiving needs
that connection let in. Neither is needed to be seen.
.PP
On macOS, with its firewall on, macOS asks the first time
.B driftwire
listens whether to accept incoming connections: allow them, or nothing
nearby can send to this Mac.
.PP
On Linux, with firewalld, let the beacon in with the service the package
installs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo firewall\-cmd \-\-permanent \-\-add\-service=driftwire
sudo firewall\-cmd \-\-reload
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fedora Workstation's default zone already lets in ports 1025 and above, TCP
and UDP. In a zone that does not (public, on most other systems), receiving
nearby also needs TCP from the local network, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo firewall\-cmd \-\-permanent \-\-add\-rich\-rule=\(aqrule family=ipv4 \e
  source address=192.168.1.0/24 port port=1024\-65535 protocol=tcp accept\(aq
.fi
.RE
.PP
With ufw (off by default on Ubuntu), the same two:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo ufw allow 47613/udp
sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 proto tcp
.fi
.RE
.PP
where 192.168.1.0/24 is the network you receive on.
.SH PRIVACY
Nothing about your files leaves the computer except to the one you send them
to, sealed. The beacon carries a random id, the name other computers see
(only while the session runs), a port and the protocol version: never a file
name or anything about a file. What the session prints stays in the
terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B driftwire send ~/Pictures/trip/*.heic
Send some photos: choose the computer, type its code.
.TP
.B driftwire receive \-\-once \-\-save\-to /tmp/in
Receive one transfer into
.IR /tmp/in ,
then close.
.TP
.B driftwire code
Make a code to give someone far away.
.SH SEE ALSO
